sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: stage 4 of the command development pipeline (feature-flow sections 3 and 4). It continues the one PR that stage 1 opened as a draft with the spec. The code and the runnable proof cases of the command are pushed to the same branch, so a reviewer can check that the chart, the docs, the cases, the code and the proof cases match. There is one CI: the fast checks plus, for command code, the proof CI (proof:run for the affected commands). It runs on every push. When everything is green the PR leaves draft; then comes the gate, and Swamp merges the PR with a Gateway-minted App token. There is no merge queue. The Gateway only admits and mints. GitHub auto-merge is not used.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 4 · code, proof CI and merge in the same PR
  %% kind: pipeline.4-pr-and-ci
  autonumber
  %% lane AG icon=terminal pill=author tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=PR tone=purple
  %% lane CI icon=workflow pill=checks tone=amber
  participant AG as Task VM<br/>author · reviewer
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>authorizes only
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>owns flow · state
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>PR · main
  participant CI as CI<br/>fast · proof CI
  AG->>GH: Push code and proof cases<br/>same branch as the draft PR
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the draft PR of stage 1 exists and its first commit holds the spec: chart, page and cases
  %%> - the cases passed on the nested fleet (stage 3)
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - pushes the command code and the runnable proof cases to the branch of the draft PR; no second PR is opened
  %%> - the chart, page, cases, code and proof cases are now in one PR, so a reviewer can check that they match
  %%> Built:
  %%> - the Swamp prep flow (`operation-task-prep`, stage 1) opens the draft PR as the Orbit App with `task:publish --draft`; 1 of 3 runs succeeded by 2026-11-06
  GH->>CI: Start CI<br/>on the PR head, every push
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - GitHub runs `ci.yml` for the PR head on every push, also while the PR is a draft; there is one CI, and the merge requires its one result
  %%> - the handoff branch `task-N-handoff`, which only the Orbit App pushes, runs the same CI before `task:publish`; its Swamp workflow validation step gets the read-only Swamp CI token for a push by the App
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
  %%> - without the token, no handoff that changes `swamp/` could pass hosted CI; the token goes to that one step, never to `pull_request_target` or a fork
  Note over CI: Scope the change<br/>changed files pick the jobs
  %%> Rules:
  %%> - documentation only (`docs/`, `*.md`, charts): docs checks only, never the proof CI; this is the spec commit alone
  %%> - Gateway code: fast checks and the Gateway Pest suite
  %%> - web or CLI code: fast checks and that build
  %%> - command code (workflows, extensions, playbooks, roles, versions): fast checks and the proof CI
  %%> - the proof CI starts only when a changed path selects a command (or `*`, every case) in `swamp/proof/selection.txt`, the same list `proof:run` reads; docs, charts and case descriptions never start it, also inside a directory that selects `*`
  %%> Decided 2026-11-06:
  %%> - the mapping is data in `selection.txt`, one block per command family: its Gateway Action, Request, Data, Domain, Infrastructure, Model, migration, route and controller files, its CLI command and vocabulary files, and its SDK request and response files select every command of the family; code that serves another family stays out, for example the router LAN ingress files
  %%> - a family's block is added with the first cases file of the family, because a path that selects only commands with no cases file starts a proof run that proves nothing; `apps/**` stays `skip` until then
  %%> - code that every command runs through selects `*`, which runs the cases of every command that has a cases file, so a shared change re-proves what it can break; the shared files are named in the list
  %%> - a file is chosen by what a change to it can affect: `OperationBudgetTable.php` (the run budget of each kind, one entry per kind, and the serve cap derived from it) is data and selects nothing; the rules that read it (`OperationRunBudgets.php`) and the kind registry stay shared. A change to a Swamp unit, a role template or the manifest touches no command and selects nothing; `everything` stays for a change whose reach is unknown, such as the selection list or the proof runner
  %%> - the proof CI job qualifies for the separate CI pool through its authenticated active ci-runner Node; `--report-only` does not grant capacity, and another proof cannot preempt CI (rule and reason: the chart of `proof:run`, `docs/cli/proof/run.mmd`)
  %%> Built:
  %%> - the firewall family (`firewall:allow`, `firewall:deny`, `firewall:list`, `firewall:remove`) and the shared files, checked by Pest tests against the checked-in list
  Note over CI: Fast checks<br/>about 2 minutes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Project check fails on formatter drift: Rector and Pint run in fix mode, and a commit that they would change fails the check, so main stays formatter-clean and `composer check` in a task rewrites only the files the task touched
  %%> - runs the required job set `Required checks`; every main commit also builds the web artifact and `Gateway release inputs` for the release (stage 5)
  opt command code changed
    opt the PR is from outside
      GH->>CI: Review + run-proofs label<br/>maintainer
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - a maintainer approves the head, then adds the label `run-proofs`; the label starts the workflow `proof-outside`, which passes that head SHA to `proof:run` on the proof runner
      %%> - the workflow runs from the base branch of the PR (`pull_request_target`) and checks out only its own script, so the PR cannot change what runs on the runner; the code of the PR runs nowhere but in the task VM, after the Gateway checked the label and a maintainer's approval of exactly that commit
      %%> Why:
      %%> - a new push removes the label (the workflow does it) and moves the head; the new head needs a new approval, then the label again
      %%> - a label added before the approval fails with `proof.commit_untrusted`; remove it and add it again after approving
      %%> Built:
      %%> - the workflow `.github/workflows/proof-outside.yml`: the job `proof-run` on `labeled` and the job `unlabel` on `synchronize`, for pull requests from a fork only
      %%> - the Gateway side, `proof:run` refusing an untrusted commit with `proof.commit_untrusted`
      %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
      %%> - a workflow of its own, not a trigger on `ci.yml`: `pull_request_target` on `ci.yml` would run every job of the PR with the secrets and the write token of the base repository
      %%> - the result is a run of that workflow; `Required checks` does not wait for it, unlike the required proof CI of a branch PR
    end
    CI->>GW: Run the proof CI<br/>proof:run COMMIT
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - a job on the beast or shark runner passes only the full SHA and an idempotency key; it checks out only its own script and chooses no cases
    %%> - a new push cancels the proof run of the previous push on the same PR (GitHub concurrency), so only the latest push finishes its proof
    %%> - the Gateway checks the commit, selects whole batches and queues CI proofs first come first served; the front proof takes every free usable CI place up to its batch count, balances by measured case time and starts one Swamp run per VM, as in the chart of `proof:run`
    %%> - the run starts a fresh task VM from the current images, mounts its checkout of the commit in the nested fleet, converges that VM once when the PR changes playbooks or pins, and takes `base`; each batch restores `base` once, nothing converges again
    %%> - the base images stay as they are; only the merge and the seed refresh (stage 6) change them
    %%> Built:
    %%> - the job `proof-run` runs only when a changed path selects a command in `selection.txt`, passes only the SHA and an idempotency key, and a new push cancels the run of the previous push; it follows the one `proof.run` record until the last command run ended
    %%> - capacity waits for up to two hours; a timeout fails clearly, and no capacity result can turn green with no case verdict
    %%> - it fails on a missing result, an outcome other than `passed` or `nothing_to_prove`, a passed proof with no case that ran, and any case that is not `passed`, such as `unrun`
    %%> Built, not yet passed live:
    %%> - when the PR adds a case, the same proof VM then switches its checkout to the merge base with the new cases file and must see every new case fail; see `Prove the case can fail` in the [stage 3 chart](/reference/command-development-pipeline#3-proof)
    %%> Built:
    %%> - the proof CI is part of Required checks: the job `proof-run` calls `proof:run`, requires every selected case verdict and fails on a failed case or missing verdict; no selected command passes
    GW-->>CI: Verdict per case
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the job reads the operation until it ends; a missing result or a failed assertion fails the CI result
  end
  alt every check passes
    CI-->>GH: One CI result<br/>green
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the merge requires this one result on the PR head
    Note over GW,GH: Start the gate check<br/>every minute · reads only
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - a Gateway schedule runs every minute, like the release check of stage 5; it reads with read-only App tokens the open external Task groups that have a published PR, and for each its PR, its CI result on the head and its reviews
    %%> - a draft PR whose CI result is green on its head, and whose head has a passed handoff check (`task:check` handoff) of the group on exactly that commit: the Gateway admits the ready step below
    %%> - a draft PR of a prep group (a succeeded `tasks.prep` run) whose build has not started, where Nick's latest review requests changes on that exact head: the Gateway admits the corrections of stage 1 (`task:prep-fix`), keyed by group and review, while the group is idle (decided by the orchestrator for Nick's review, see `1-prep.mmd`)
    %%> - a PR out of draft in manual mode, where Nick's latest review approves that exact head: the Gateway admits the merge step below
    %%> - a PR out of draft in automatic mode, with no review round recorded for that head: the Gateway admits the review round below
    %%> - a PR out of draft in automatic mode, whose round on that exact head passed: the Gateway admits the merge step below
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the Gateway is private and its App receives no webhooks, so a short poll sees the green CI and the approval
    %%> - Swamp steps make no Gateway calls; each admission starts its own Swamp run with the full intent
    %%> Rules:
    %%> - admission is keyed by group and head, so a repeat tick on the same head is a no-op; a push moves the head and the check starts again. The corrections of a spec are keyed by group and review instead: each new review of the owner starts one run, and a failed run starts again only with a new review
    %%> - a pending or red CI result, a missing approval, a busy group or a failed read is a quiet skip; the next tick reads again
    %%> - a draft PR with no passed handoff check on its head stays a draft, because the code and the runnable cases are not there yet: the PR that `task:prep` opens for a spec is such a PR, and an approval never merges it
    %%> - only a Project with review and merge enabled takes part
    %%> Built:
    %%> - the check for both modes, as the scheduled command `task:pr-gate`; the review round of the first automatic-mode rule, the merge after a passed round, and the fix after findings
    %%> - the admission of the corrections of a prep spec, for an idle prep group whose owner requested changes on the current head
    GW->>SW: Admit the ready step<br/>token key name
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - authorizes the step and names the vault key of its token; the step mints the token when it runs
    %%> When:
    %%> - the PR is a draft and its CI is green on the head
    SW->>GH: Take the PR out of draft<br/>as the Orbit App
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - marks the PR ready for review with the App token, passed by vault key name
    %%> - in manual mode, also requests Nick's review, so GitHub notifies him that the PR waits for him
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a draft means the work is still moving; leaving draft says the spec, code and proof cases are done
    %%> Built:
    %%> - the operation `task:pr-ready`, with its chart, called by the gate check
    %%> - the call in automatic mode, where it requests no review; after the fall back to manual mode the step runs again and requests it
    alt manual mode
      GW->>GH: Read the PR reviews<br/>short poll · approval on head
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - Nick's approving review on the exact PR head is the gate; the Gateway sees it with a token that has only `pull_requests: read`
      %%> Why:
      %%> - the Gateway is private and its App receives no webhooks, so a short poll sees the approval
      %%> - a push moves the PR head, so the approval must follow the last push
      %%> Built:
      %%> - the read, as the gate of the operation `task:pr-merge`, polled every minute by the gate check
      GH-->>GW: Approval on the head
    else automatic mode
      GW->>SW: Admit a review round<br/>group · head · round N
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - one Swamp run per round, admitted by the gate check, keyed by group and head; the Gateway records the round number on the group
      %%> - the mode is set per Task group when it is created (`task:create --review=automatic`); the default is manual
      %%> Built:
      %%> - the mode of the group, set at creation and shown by `task:show` as `review`
      %%> - the admission, keyed by group and head, with the round number recorded on the group; it needs a green merge check and a passed handoff candidate on the head
      %%> - the fall back to manual mode after round 3 with findings, or when a round fails
      %%> - the one Swamp run per round, `tasks.review_round`, as the chart in docs/cli/task/review-round.mmd shows it: the reviewer turn, the comment, and the fix with its check and push are steps of that run, and the gate check admits nothing else for a round
      SW->>AG: Review the PR head<br/>fresh reviewer turn
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - a child run of the existing turn workflow with the role `reviewer`, in a new thread: an agent that took no part in the spec, the code or earlier rounds
      %%> - it runs in the task VM, which the run claims afresh if the previous VM was released; it reads the PR head, the chart, the page, the cases and the CI result, and judges them against the same review rules as Nick
      %%> - the verdict is pass, or findings with file and reason; it is posted as a PR comment by the App so Nick can read it
      %%> Built:
      %%> - the comment, as steps of the round run: it mints its own token when it runs, and a comment that GitHub refuses does not fail the run
      %%> Why:
      %%> - the App opened the PR, and GitHub does not let an author approve its own PR, so the pass is recorded by the Gateway, not as a GitHub approval
      AG-->>SW: Pass<br/>or findings
      opt findings
        SW->>AG: Fix the findings<br/>author turn · push
        %%> What it does:
        %%> - a child run of the turn workflow with the role `implementer`, in the author's thread, with the findings as its prompt
        %%> - then the handoff check and a push to the same branch, as the publish step does; the new head gets a new CI run, and the gate check admits the next round once it is green
        %%> Built:
        %%> - the three steps, as child runs of the round run (the turn, check and publish workflows); a step that fails or ends without a pass leaves findings without a pushed commit, and the gate check sends the group back to manual mode
      end
      SW-->>GW: Round result<br/>terminal event
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the Gateway records pass or findings for that head and round
      %%> - a pass on the head lets the gate check admit the merge step
      %%> - after round 3 with findings, the Gateway switches the group to manual mode; the ready step's review request goes to Nick, and the PR waits for his approval
    GW->>SW: Admit the merge step<br/>fresh token key name
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a token lives one hour and an approval can take longer, so the merge step mints a new one when it runs
    SW->>GH: Merge the PR<br/>squash · App token
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - Swamp merges the PR straight into main with a squash merge, using the App token that the step minted, passed by vault key name
    %%> - the Orbit App, through this Swamp step, decides whether the PR may merge; the PR's CI is green on its head and the gate has passed
    %%> - a merge conflict with main refuses the merge; the author rebases, which reruns CI and the proof, and the gate starts again
    %%> History:
    %%> - no merge queue and no GitHub auto-merge (Nick, 2026-11-05): the queue only reran the 2-minute fast checks, because the proof is not run twice
    %%> Built:
    %%> - the operation `task:pr-merge`, with its chart, called by the gate check
    %%> - the call in automatic mode, after a passed round on the head
    %%> - a PR that no Task group watches merges through `project:pr-merge` (`docs/cli/project/pr-merge.mmd`): the same Swamp step, admitted by the Gateway for the Project, with the green merge check on the exact head as the gate
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
    %%> - for a PR outside a Task group the authorized caller is the reviewer, so no review is read; the gate is the green merge check on the exact head, a PR that a Task group watches is refused (`task:pr-merge` owns it), and so is a PR that does not target the default branch or that comes from a fork (a fork can define its own workflow that reports a passing merge check; open for Nick: how to trust a fork)
    GH->>CI: CI on main<br/>after the merge
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the normal CI run of the new main commit; the Gateway release (stage 5) takes only a green one
    %%> When it fails:
    %%> - two green PRs can still break main together; main is fixed forward, and no release takes the red commit
  else a check fails
    CI--xGH: One CI result<br/>red
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the PR stays a draft and is not merged; the author fixes and pushes again, and CI runs again on the new head
    %%> - a PR that left draft and then goes red is fixed the same way, and the gate starts again on the new head
  end
